Well, be careful.

One big deficit of NetHack-as-is is its inability to
play in tiles mode across a remote (home computer to
server) interface, because it is communicating
display specific information across that interface
that assumes a telnet-style display on the user end.

That pretty much puts those who play exclusively in
tiles mode out of the running for tournaments.

You sort of _must_ factor the program interface into
a display neutral part that communicates the screen
changes needed in a single form irrespective of
display mode in use at the user end, and so do-able
across a remote interface, and a local part that
translates those changes into display-specific
functionality that changes the display chosen by the
player in an appropriate way.

The reverse communication also needs to work
regardless of the display the user is seeing, so
that pointing a mouse at a spot on the screen and
clicking translates back from whatever tile size is
in use, and from however the tiles are presently
panned in 2D on the screen, into a click on a
particular game internals grid cell, and
communicates that across the display functionality
to game internals engine interface.

If you leave all those considerations out of the
game design at the start, you'll have a real mess
retro-installing them later.

The Crawl two level display (local cells large,
whole level cells 2x2 pixels) wouldn't translate
well to pure text mode, but nothing prevents the
whole game being played in tiles mode, with ASCII
(or wide curses Unicode) glyph tiles as on of the
optional tile sets.

